=== SCALABILITY / TIMING TEST (not a spec requirement — extra depth for report §12) ===
Measured with `date +%s%N` around a batch of concurrent clients, run inside the
css223-reservation Docker image. All runs: sync=ON (mutex enabled).

--- Test 1: does adding workers help RESERVE throughput? ---
20 clients, each RESERVE a distinct resource (1-20), workers varied:

  workers=1   clients=20  elapsed=5817ms
  workers=3   clients=20  elapsed=6398ms
  workers=10  clients=20  elapsed=6180ms

FINDING: worker count barely matters (~6s regardless of 1, 3, or 10 workers).
Cause: `table_mutex` is ONE global lock covering the entire resource map, and
`random_delay()` (50-500ms) runs INSIDE that lock in handle_reserve(). Every
RESERVE — even on a completely different resource_id — must wait for the
current lock holder's full delay to finish. With 20 successful reservations
each holding the lock for ~50-500ms (avg ~275ms), total time is bounded below
by roughly 20 x 275ms =~ 5.5s no matter how many worker threads exist, because
they all queue up on the same mutex. More workers only helps once something
is actually parallel; here nothing is, once you're inside handle_reserve.

--- Test 2: does client load affect STATUS (read-only) throughput? ---
workers fixed at 3, client count varied, each client sends one STATUS:

  clients=5    elapsed=5ms
  clients=20   elapsed=10ms
  clients=50   elapsed=20ms
  clients=200  elapsed=57ms

FINDING: scales roughly linearly and stays fast even at 200 concurrent clients
— no cliff/breakdown observed in this range. Cause: STATUS's critical section
is just a map lookup (no random_delay), so table_mutex is held for
microseconds, not hundreds of milliseconds — contention is real but brief.

--- Kernel-level ceiling (context, not hit in testing) ---
`ipcs -l` on this image: default max queue size = 16384 bytes. Each message is
~524 bytes, so roughly ~31 unconsumed messages can sit in the queue before a
client's msgsnd (no IPC_NOWAIT) blocks waiting for a worker to drain it. Not
observed as a bottleneck up to 200 concurrent STATUS clients — workers drained
the queue faster than it filled, since each STATUS is serviced in
microseconds.

--- Honest methodology caveats ---
- Each "client" here is a full process spawned via Bash inside `docker exec`;
  at very high counts (thousands), process-spawn overhead itself would start
  to dominate the measured time, not the server's own performance. These
  numbers describe server behavior in the tested range (up to 200 clients),
  not a rigorous load-testing tool's output.
- No genuine breakdown/crash point was found within this range — the numbers
  above describe the server's behavior, not its failure mode.

--- Take-away for the report (§12 — limitations / future improvements) ---
The bottleneck this system will hit first under load is NOT worker count, and
not message-queue capacity — it is the fact that ALL resources share a single
global mutex, and the deliberately-added experiment delay is held inside it.
A future improvement: one mutex per resource (or a fixed-size array of
mutexes) instead of one for the whole table, so reservations on different
seats can proceed fully in parallel. This system currently trades reservation
throughput for the simplicity of a single lock, which is a reasonable choice
for a 20-seat/5-client class assignment but would not scale to a
larger/production system.
